WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.720
At 43,000 workers left Kenya's reserves for coffee estates and food plots stayed empty when

00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:05.680
prices collapsed.

00:00:06.180 --> 00:00:09.580
This is unintended consequences, episode 184.

00:00:10.280 --> 00:00:16.760
In 1930, the land planted to European coffee and Kenya reached 96,042 acres, more than

00:00:16.760 --> 00:00:21.120
triple the 33,813 acres recorded in 1921.

00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:26.520
Between those years, the crop spread with newest state infrastructure around Thika and Kiyambu.

00:00:26.520 --> 00:00:33.420
the 1927 harvest reached 12,300 tons. Colonial officials had introduced minimum

00:00:33.420 --> 00:00:37.560
price guarantees after the First World War so settlers would expand the crop.

00:00:38.320 --> 00:00:43.960
After the 1919-1921 price slump, administrators wanted to steady and enlarge

00:00:43.960 --> 00:00:48.040
the settler coffee sector, the export they regarded as the colony's most

00:00:48.040 --> 00:00:53.400
promising. The 1923 dual policy statement and earlier recommendations of the

00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:58.780
1912 Native Labor Commission framed the effort as a way to secure steady revenue while

00:00:58.780 --> 00:01:01.580
still preserving African reserves for subsistence.

00:01:02.100 --> 00:01:06.400
Officials believed a price floor would give settlers the confidence to clear more land

00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:08.460
and hire for a labor-intensive crop.

00:01:09.120 --> 00:01:14.320
Those officials expected the wages to supplement African household incomes without removing workers

00:01:14.320 --> 00:01:15.560
from the reserves for good.

00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:22.540
The same administrators imposed hot taxes, and from 1924, the Kipanday Registration System

00:01:22.540 --> 00:01:25.600
to channel labor toward the estates on a seasonal basis.

00:01:26.340 --> 00:01:30.960
The measures appeared rational to men who had watched wartime shortages and post-war

00:01:30.960 --> 00:01:33.040
volatility threaten the colonial budget.

00:01:33.660 --> 00:01:38.520
In their view, a guaranteed return on coffee would anchor the settler economy without permanent

00:01:38.520 --> 00:01:40.260
transfers of land or people.

00:01:40.940 --> 00:01:45.700
The administrators calculated that seasonal flows would let workers keep plots at home in

00:01:45.700 --> 00:01:46.560
off-peak months.

00:01:47.160 --> 00:01:51.420
That subsistence base they thought would keep feeding the estates and supplying future

00:01:51.420 --> 00:01:56.880
recruits. The arithmetic seemed straightforward. Higher coffee revenue would cover administrative

00:01:56.880 --> 00:02:01.900
costs, while taxes and passes kept the labor supply flexible rather than fixed.

00:02:02.740 --> 00:02:07.440
Wage rates for pickers settled at roughly one shilling a day on many estates, a figure

00:02:07.440 --> 00:02:12.240
that drew Kikuyu and other workers from the reserves. The Native Labor Commission had

00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:17.300
already named labor shortages as the chief obstacle, so the price policy was paired with

00:02:17.300 --> 00:02:23.180
tighter tax collection and the Kepande Pass system. By 1930, the number of permanent African

00:02:23.180 --> 00:02:30.420
workers on European estates had climbed from 15,216. Contemporary reports from district officers

00:02:30.420 --> 00:02:35.140
noted that the flow of men to the coffee zones eased the labor bottleneck and allowed further

00:02:35.140 --> 00:02:40.040
planting. At the time, few colonial records questioned whether the movement might become

00:02:40.040 --> 00:02:46.020
permanent once prices changed. Estate managers in Kiyambu and Thika reported that tax pressure

00:02:46.020 --> 00:02:50.420
and registration made recruitment reliable enough to justify clearing additional acres

00:02:50.420 --> 00:02:51.180
each season.

00:02:52.132 --> 00:02:57.492
When world coffee prices dropped from 22.5 cents to 8 cents a pound during the Great Depression,

00:02:58.212 --> 00:03:00.972
many estates reduced operations or dismissed workers.

00:03:01.612 --> 00:03:05.772
The same men who had left their subsistence plots found themselves without land rights

00:03:05.772 --> 00:03:10.572
in the reserves, and without the skills or capital to re-enter commercial farming elsewhere.

00:03:10.572 --> 00:03:15.132
Research published in the Southern Journal for Contemporary History traces how

00:03:15.132 --> 00:03:19.332
hot-tacks, the Kippon Day, and a state recruitment had already eroded the

00:03:19.332 --> 00:03:24.112
capacity of reserve households to maintain food production. As documented in

00:03:24.112 --> 00:03:28.992
the 2019 economic history of developing regions paper, the shift of labor to

00:03:28.992 --> 00:03:33.132
high-value coffee estates reduced the area under maze and other staples in the

00:03:33.132 --> 00:03:38.172
Kikuyu reserves. Workers who remained near Nairobi or the estate towns after the

00:03:38.172 --> 00:03:41.252
collapse had no automatic route back to viable farming.

00:03:41.852 --> 00:03:47.172
The book Migrant Labor in Kenya shows that taxation and land policy had converted temporary

00:03:47.172 --> 00:03:51.172
wage spells into longer-term dependence on urban or estate employment.

00:03:51.992 --> 00:03:56.572
Reduced food output in the reserves raised local prices and increased reliance on imported

00:03:56.572 --> 00:03:57.092
maize.

00:03:57.552 --> 00:04:02.012
The growing pool of under-employed migrants in towns altered household structures and

00:04:02.012 --> 00:04:04.152
limited investment in reserve agriculture.

00:04:04.152 --> 00:04:11.152
The 1975 monograph Colonial Capitalism and Labor in Kenya records that employer wage controls

00:04:11.152 --> 00:04:16.652
introduced to keep coffee profitable, further discouraged workers from returning even when a

00:04:16.652 --> 00:04:22.692
state demand fell. These link changes reallocated labor away from the subsistence sector the original

00:04:22.692 --> 00:04:27.772
price support had been intended to complement. Households that once rotated members between

00:04:27.772 --> 00:04:33.672
estates and reserves now faced a permanent split. One earner stayed near wage work. The remaining

00:04:33.672 --> 00:04:36.692
remaining members struggled with smaller plots and higher food costs.

00:04:37.292 --> 00:04:41.992
Over successive seasons, the loss of able-bodied labor meant less land cleared for staples,

00:04:42.612 --> 00:04:46.932
fewer seeds saved, and weaker claims to communal grazing or water rights back home.

00:04:47.692 --> 00:04:52.252
District reports noted rising arrears on hut taxes among those who stayed in the reserves.

00:04:52.992 --> 00:04:57.052
Those arrears triggered further recruitment drives that pulled still more people outward.

00:04:57.592 --> 00:05:02.132
The pattern compounded because the Kepande system recorded workers' movements but offered

00:05:02.132 --> 00:05:06.972
no mechanism for restoring land rights once a man had been absent for several tax cycles.

00:05:07.892 --> 00:05:12.872
After the price collapse, colonial authorities did not restore the earlier price floor for coffee.

00:05:13.492 --> 00:05:18.272
The authorities turned instead to marketing boards, and later, to direct subsidies during

00:05:18.272 --> 00:05:22.932
the emergency period. The permanent movement of workers out of the reserves continued,

00:05:23.452 --> 00:05:29.132
documented in studies of Kikuyu Squatter Labor through the 1930s. No subsequent policy

00:05:29.132 --> 00:05:33.412
reverse the land and registration rules that had facilitated the initial outflow.

00:05:33.972 --> 00:05:38.672
By the late colonial years, the pattern of rural to urban and rural to estate migration

00:05:38.672 --> 00:05:42.932
had become self-reinforcing, shaping post-independence labor markets.

00:05:43.492 --> 00:05:48.292
The structural separation between reserve agriculture and wage employment persisted long

00:05:48.292 --> 00:05:50.532
after the original price guarantees ended.

00:05:51.212 --> 00:05:55.912
Later attempts to promote African cash crop growing inside the reserves operated against

00:05:55.912 --> 00:06:01.272
the background of this earlier labor drain, and the fragmented households that drain left behind.

00:06:02.207 --> 00:06:07.627
A price guarantee tied to one crop and one set of employers rewards the reallocation of labor

00:06:07.627 --> 00:06:13.007
toward that crop even after the guarantee is withdrawn. In Kenya, the combination of tax,

00:06:13.327 --> 00:06:18.307
registration, and wage policy made the shift from reserves to a state's cheaper to enter than to

00:06:18.307 --> 00:06:24.107
reverse. The exit path has to be designed with the recruitment mechanism, because later adjustments

00:06:24.107 --> 00:06:27.387
rarely restore the original balance of household resources.

00:06:28.027 --> 00:06:32.867
Listeners who follow price supports as investments can hear the same tension on modern investing

00:06:32.867 --> 00:06:37.727
techniques, where a floor that looks like security can still lock people into one market.

00:06:38.347 --> 00:06:42.667
If a government today guarantees a return on a single export and pairs that guarantee

00:06:42.667 --> 00:06:47.747
with taxes or passes that pull workers toward it, what exit would you insist on seeing

00:06:47.747 --> 00:06:49.927
written down before the first season of hiring?

00:06:50.607 --> 00:06:51.807
That's the case for today.

00:06:51.807 --> 00:06:56.487
The lessons tend to travel further when you share them, so send this one to someone who'd

00:06:56.487 --> 00:06:59.347
find it useful, and subscribe wherever you're listening.

